---
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_URL } from "../config/site";
import { FEATURED_BOOK } from "../data/books";

const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_URL}/about/`;
const description = `About ${SITE_NAME} — how these books are written, who they are for, and what they promise.`;
const book = FEATURED_BOOK;
---

<Layout
	title={`About — ${SITE_NAME}`}
	description={description}
	canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
	active="about"
>
	<div class="page page-narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">About</span>
		<hr class="rule-short" />
		<h1>Simon Rook</h1>

		<div class="prose" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
			<p class="lede">
				These books are about one problem: what a thoughtful person should do when
				careful thinking stops producing answers.
			</p>

			<p>
				That problem has a particular shape. It rarely feels like avoidance. It feels like
				diligence — replaying the conversation to get it right, comparing the options one
				more time, waiting one more night before deciding. The effort is real. It just
				doesn't finish, because the missing answer belongs to another person, to an event
				that hasn't happened, or to a future no amount of review can reach.
			</p>

			<p>
				Ancient philosophy turns out to be unusually good on this, and Stoicism most of
				all. The Stoics did not live with email, performance reviews, or an endless supply
				of online opinion, but the mind they studied is the same one. They separated an
				event from our judgment about it. They distinguished what depends on our choices
				from what stays exposed to chance and other people. They asked how a person could
				act well while outcomes remained uncertain — which is the only condition anyone
				ever actually acts in.
			</p>

			<h2>How these books are written</h2>

			<p>
				They are practical before they are historical. Each one starts from a recognizable
				situation rather than an ancient quotation, introduces one idea at a time in plain
				language, and is explicit about what that idea does <em>not</em> mean. Every
				chapter ends with something to actually do.
			</p>

			<p>
				Where ancient texts are quoted, they are checked against named editions and cited
				in endnotes, with public-domain translations preferred. Where an old translation is
				needlessly difficult, it is paraphrased and labelled as such. Modern applications
				are marked as adaptations rather than presented as what a Stoic said.
			</p>

			<h2>What they will not do</h2>

			<ul class="feature-list">
				<li>
					<Icon name="lucide:minus" aria-hidden="true" />
					<span>
						Present Stoicism as emotional suppression, or as becoming indifferent to
						people and outcomes you care about.
					</span>
				</li>
				<li>
					<Icon name="lucide:minus" aria-hidden="true" />
					<span>
						Make medical or therapeutic promises, or suggest that philosophy is a
						substitute for qualified care.
					</span>
				</li>
				<li>
					<Icon name="lucide:minus" aria-hidden="true" />
					<span>
						Treat calm as the point. Calm is a possible benefit of Stoic practice, not
						its aim and not its definition of a life well lived.
					</span>
				</li>
				<li>
					<Icon name="lucide:minus" aria-hidden="true" />
					<span>Wander into politics, religious framing, or inspirational quotation collecting.</span>
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Who they are for</h2>

			<p>
				Adults who are conscientious and analytical, who take things seriously, and who
				have noticed that taking things seriously has started to cost them decisions. No
				prior knowledge of philosophy is assumed anywhere.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="button-row section-sm">
			<a class="btn btn-primary" href={`/books/${book.slug}/`}>
				<Icon name="lucide:book-open" aria-hidden="true" />
				Read about the book
			</a>
			<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>
		</div>
	</div>
</Layout>
